Big O Analysis
Operation	Vector	      Hash     Table	    Tree (Balanced)
Loading Data	          O(n)	   O(n)	      O(nlogn)
Printing Sorted List	  O(nlogn) O(nlogn)	  O(n)
Finding a Single Course	O(n)	   O(1)	      O(logn)
________________________________________
Explanation of Runtimes
•	Loading Data:
o	Vector and Hash Table: Both offer an average-case O(1) insertion. Performing this for n courses results in an overall linear runtime of O(n).
o	Tree: Inserting each of the n courses into a balanced binary search tree requires traversing the tree to find the correct spot, which takes O(logn) time per insertion. This leads to a total loading time of O(nlogn).
•	Printing Sorted List:
o	Vector and Hash Table: Neither data structure maintains sorted order. To print the list in alphanumeric order, the data must first be sorted, which is a process with a worst-case runtime of O(nlogn).
o	Tree: A binary search tree is naturally ordered. An in-order traversal visits every node exactly once and does so in sorted order, making the operation highly efficient at O(n).
•	Finding a Single Course:
o	Vector: A linear search is required, which in the worst case must check every course, resulting in an O(n) runtime.
o	Hash Table: By using a hash function, the location of a course can be found in average constant time, O(1).
o	Tree: Searching in a balanced tree is done by eliminating half of the nodes at each level, similar to a binary search. The time complexity is proportional to the height of the tree, which is O(logn).
