Evaluation and Recommendation
Evaluation of Data Structures
•	Vector:
o	Advantages: Simple to implement and has a fast loading time of O(n).
o	Disadvantages: Inefficient for both key project requirements: searching for a course (O(n)) and printing a sorted list (O(nlogn)).
•	Hash Table:
o	Advantages: Extremely fast for single-course lookups (O(1) on average), making it the most efficient option for this task. It also has a fast loading time of O(n).
o	Disadvantages: It does not preserve order, requiring an expensive O(nlogn) sorting step to print an alphanumeric course list.
•	Binary Search Tree:
o	Advantages: Efficiently handles both of the project's main requirements. Searching is fast (O(logn)), and printing a sorted list is the most efficient of the three (O(n)). This makes it a great choice for applications where both operations are common.
o	Disadvantages: The loading process is slower than the other two structures (O(nlogn)), and the implementation is more complex.
Recommendation
I recommend using a binary search tree for this project.
The justification is based on its balanced performance across all requirements. While the hash table offers a faster search time, the tree's ability to provide a sorted list in linear time (O(n)) is a significant advantage. Given that the project requires both efficient course lookup and the ability to print an ordered list, the binary search tree provides the optimal balance. Its O(logn) search time is more than sufficient, and its superior performance for the sorted print function makes it the best overall choice.

